t  on the left when it's close to the stool  it creates a pretty small  spot that's very bright on on the stool  in the next one the flashlight we've  moved it a bit farther away  and the spot gets bigger it also gets a  little bit dimmer you can start seeing  some of the grain of the wood  where the light's hitting and the third  one to the right we've  moved the flashlight very far away from  the stool  it's lit up the stool but now the entire  stool is in frame  and for the most part you can see all  the grain so  the the light has lost some of its  brightness as well as  spread out in addition to moving the  light away  you can also try bouncing the light so  instead of for example with a camera  flash  so in the leftmost image here we have  light that's going to go directly  from the flash to the subject and that's  going to be a very  hard light very bright and hard light  or instead of directing it straight  towards the subject  in the second image we can point the  flash straight up to the ceiling  now when that light instead of now going  directly to the subject  goes up to the ceiling spreads out  and then lights the entire subject  evenly  so by bouncing the light instead of  sending it straight towards your subject  that can help in creating softer light  if you don't have a flash for your  camera or using a cell phone  another thing you can do is use a piece  of paper  so i recommend white paper because  that's going to reflect  most of the light and what i'm doing  here is  lighting this dragon you can actually  see it the light  you can see how much brighter the front  of the dragon is compared to the rest of  the stool  and what's happening here is the  flashlight is bouncing  off of this paper and then spreading out  and hitting the dragon so if you don't  have  a ceiling close by if your ceiling is  really high up or if it's a darker color  you may want to use a piece of paper and  if you don't have a flash use a  flashlight and it works  just as well we can also  modify our flash or our lights in order  to  spread out the light and these are  called  diffusers and diffusers just it's  something that the light passes  through so that it spreads out before  hitting your  subject so there are ones that you can  put on here like in the second image to  the left here  just plastic pieces that can go on a  camera flash or  you don't have that just use a piece of  paper so in the third image here we have  just a piece of paper taped to the front  of the flash and now  the light from the flash has to pass  through that paper and spread out  before hitting our subject if you want  to aim that light a little bit more  just change the shape of the paper so in  this last image to the right here  we have the paper curled around the  flash and so now when the light comes  out it's going to bounce off this paper  and then  be bounced forward towards the subject  in front of the camera  we can also do this with lamps so a  simple way to turn  a lamp light into a more diffused light  is something that the lamp probably  already has is a shade  so instead of pointing the lamp directly  at your subject  have the light that hits your subject  come from the side of the lamp where the  light is has to pass through the  lampshade  before hitting the subject you can also  use the same paper techniques on the  bottom right here  i've taped a piece of paper right in  front of that lamp  and that spreads out the light so much  more  of the subject has light falling on him  rather than just that one spot that we  had before we added that piece of paper  so now that you can control hard light  and soft light and turn hard light into  soft light  let's look at another factor that can  greatly change  what your image looks like and this is  the background so you can change the  background  of your image and that's going to have a  large effect on how the light  bounces off of your subject  so in this first example i have the  dragon here and instead of shining light  directly onto the dragon  i'm shooting the light into the mirror  and then from the mirror  it's hitting the dragon and you can see  that you get in this case i'm using a  flat  uh camera flash so it has a rectangular  shape  and that mirror is going to concentrate  that light just on that one spot  so by using a mirror i reflect light  it's not really going to spread out that  much but i can highlight a very specific  portion of my subject  now if instead of a mirror what if i put  something  black as my background so here i just  have a black  box and again i'm shining the light  from the left side of my image so the  lights coming from kind of the lower  left here  and instead of hitting the dragon first  it goes to the box  and bounces back now to see what this  black box is actually doing  let's compare it with an image with a  white background  so in this third picture i've switched  out the background for a white piece of  paper  now when the light comes in it hits that  paper  reflects and spreads out and you can see  how this allows the entire  subject to be lit where when we have the  black background  only part of the subject basically where  the light happens to hit the dragon  before bouncing into the black  background  is lit up and this is because black  when the color black is formed from  reflected light  it's formed because that surface is  absorbing a lot of that light  very little light is bouncing off when  you see white  white when caused by reflected light is  caused when  all of the colors of light bounce off  that  surface and it looks white to your eye  so because white reflects light  the light bounces off that hits the  subject and we get a very  bright image if we use a black  background it's going to absorb that  light  and keep those shadows on the subject  and if you use something very reflective  like a mirror and this is going to  change a little bit depending on the  shape of the mirror  you can control exactly where that  reflected light  falls we can also play around with a  similar effect by changing the surface  um that your subject is on  so here i've taken a picture with the  exact same setup same  lighting of this glass turtle on white  paper  a wooden table and a black background  and you can see there's some dramatic  differences in these pictures even  though the light source  in this case a hard light is the same  so when i use a white background this is  going to reflect a lot of light so you  can see  that light is kind of scattering within  the clear parts  of our image these the clear parts of  the turtle are actually very bright  you can see the shadow of the turtle you  can actually see some of the color  from the light that's passing through  parts of the turtle those colors are on  that background  when i use the wood background because  that doesn't reflect as much light  we still get a shadow not as much  light now though is passing through so  the clear parts of  our turtle are actually appearing a bit  darker which is making some of the color  stand out a little bit more  but because it's not so reflective we  don't actually see any of the color  in the shadow from the turtle  and the last image here on the right  we're using a black background now the  black background absorbs light  it's also hiding the shadow so maybe i  don't want shadow in my image  so this is going to hide that it's also  going to absorb that light so we're not  getting light  bouncing back through so you can see  you get a lot less glare as the light  passes  back through the clear parts of the  turtle because the light isn't passing  back through you're actually seeing some  higher contrast between the clear  parts of the glass and the color parts  and those colors  really stand out so now what if you're  outside the sun is really bright and you  don't have another light source  well you can still control the light  that's bouncing off your subject and the  way you're going to do that is  decide on what time of day you're going  to shoot  so for example here are two images  taken arches national park and  they have very different colors and very  different lighting situations  the picture on the left was taken in the  middle of the day  the sun was really bright in the sky  it's acting like  hard light and everything is just bright  there's very little contrast meaning  everything is just kind of the same  brightness in the image there really  aren't any  dark areas if there were there would be  very dark shadows but it's very just  it's bright or it's really dark  on the right side of the image this  image was taken  near sunset and this is what's called  golden hour golden hour is either  right around sunset or right around  sunrise  when the sun is at a very low angle in  the sky  when this happens the light has to  scatter in the atmosphere the light  from the sun travels through the  atmosphere and has to spread out over a  farther distance  so not only do you get a softer light so  you can see things in the image aren't  just as bright um as in the image on the  left  but we also get some colors is that  light refracts through the atmosphere  we're getting the light separating and  we get much  more color out of her image we're  getting this reddish color  and we also see not just things that are  really bright  or really dark but we also have kind of  everything in between here  in this bottom set of images we have  the same picture taken at not just three  different times of day  but also different weather conditions  the first one on the left here  this was taken in the middle of the day  so again really hard light so things are  either really bright  or really dark if they're in shadow  in the second picture in the middle it  was really cloudy and what clouds do  is they act just like the diffusers that  we talked about earlier  so now the light from the sun has to  pass through the clouds  it bounces around it spreads out and we  get much more  even lighting and you can see  things aren't all the same brightness  now we can actually see a lot of the  colors starting to come out we have  dark blues and greens and browns in this  image because the light  is much softer in the third image on the  right  this was taken in same location but  closer to sunset near golden hour  again you can see the higher contrast so  lots of different colors lots of  different brightness  and the more of the reddish tint as the  sun refracts through the atmosphere  now taking pictures of the same subject  at different times of day  this doesn't just work for for  landscapes  but it also can really help for close-up  photos  so here isn't two different images of  lichen on a tree one of them the one on  the left was taken in the middle of the  day so we had really hard light from the  sun  and that just makes everything really  really bright  the one on the right was taken during  golden hour so taken near sunset  the light is much softer we have a much  bigger range of contrast so things are  bright  dark and in the middle and this lets us  see a lot more  detail in our subjects if you look  closely  you can see all the little kind of  spikes and nodules coming out of the  lichen on the image on the right  where the image on the left is much  harder to see so everything kind of  blends together  when everything is just lit at the same  brightness  thank you for joining us for this  tutorial in our imaging  science series with the mit museum and  coke institute  we'd love to see your stunning science  photos if you'd like to share them with  us you can share them on facebook  twitter and instagram and use the  mitm imaging science to share those with  us  at the end of this program so on june  20th we'll be holding a webinar  to look at what makes a great science  photo  and some of the so submissions have a  chance to  be featured during that webinar  for more information on the imaging  science program  other tutorials and other information  and resources  please visit the mit museum website at  the link below  and you will find a list of our other  resources as part of the imaging science  program
 

